{"key":"fca6147f-6460-445a-9e6a-5ebc5eddf703","installationKey":"a957a663-2f17-415f-b1c8-5cf6398df8ed","publishingOrganizationKey":"9b049850-d958-11da-9047-b8a03c50a862","doi":"10.15468/wnxczx","external":false,"numConstituents":0,"type":"OCCURRENCE","title":"University of Nebraska State Museum Herpetology Collection","description":"The collection of amphibians and reptiles was salvaged, organized, and cataloged by Dr. John D. Lynch prior to being moved into UNSM in the early 1970s. Early collections include those of Lawrence Bruner from Nebraska in the 1880s and Argentina in the 1890s. G.E Hudson collected extensively in the 1930s and produced &#34;The Amphibians and Reptiles of Nebraska&#34; (1942). Lynch and others continued collecting and depositing regional specimens to the collections. Royce Ballinger, Lynch, and Geoffrey Smith  produced &#34;Amphibians and Reptiles of Nebraska&#34; (2010). Also, distributions in Dan Fogell’s 2010 &#34;A Field Guide to the Amphibians and Reptiles of Nebraska&#34; are based largely on these collections. In 1999 the amphibian and reptile collections of the University of South Dakota were transferred to and incorporated into the UNSM collections. The collection today totals over 18,900 specimens, primarily from Nebraska and South Dakota.","language":"eng","homepage":"https://museum.unl.edu/collections/","citation":{"text":"Labedz T (2021). University of Nebraska State Museum Herpetology Collection. University of Nebraska State Museum. Occurrence dataset https://doi.org/10.15468/wnxczx accessed via GBIF.org on 2026-10-26.","citationProvidedBySource":false},"contactsCitation":[{"key":2327436,"abbreviatedName":"Labedz T","firstName":"Thomas","lastName":"Labedz","roles":["METADATA_AUTHOR","ORIGINATOR"],"userId":[]}],"lockedForAutoUpdate":false,"createdBy":"9b049850-d958-11da-9047-b8a03c50a862","modifiedBy":"crawler.gbif.org","created":"2021-02-24T21:47:35.322+00:00","modified":"2021-02-24T21:48:37.393+00:00","contacts":[{"key":232743